{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570284220,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU1MDI5NjcyOTg=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3752","origin":"github"},"title":"Fate of logging of results","message":"At present, any result that passes through the core event loop and has a logger instance attached, get a corresponding log message. This leads to generally duplicate reporting. For example:\n\n```\n% datalad save\n[ERROR  ]   that3: setFileMode: permission denied (Operation not permitted) [add(/tmp/perm/that3)] \nadd(error): that3 (file) [  that3: setFileMode: permission denied (Operation not permitted)]\n```\n\nI propose to demote all log messages triggered like this to a level less than `info`, hence remove them from the default user experience.\n\nRational: \"error/impossible\"-type events can be caused by non-faulty probing behavior and those should not clutter the terminal with scary inconsequential messages. Still logging any of such events at a lower level keeps them accessible for debugging. In addition, any faulty behavior that only leads to an \"error\" log message, without a corresponding \"error\" result is likely a bug (e.g. not leading to non-zero exit code, on-failure handling not controllable, etc.).\n\nIf that is not acceptable, an alternative solution to a cleaner UX is to no longer add a logger to the result records -- but that is clearly inferior for a developer perspective.","files":null}]}